# Fingerprints of third-party instrument text that must never enter this repository.
#
# The 62 politicalcompass.org propositions are NOT the author's work. They are licensed
# third-party text, and they appear verbatim in the `forcing_prompt` field of every run
# record in the private study directory. On 2026-09-01, 525 such run files were copied into
# this repository's working tree while staging a data release, 460 of them carrying the text.
# Nothing in .gitignore stood between them and a `git add -A`.
#
# scripts/check_corpus.py greps staged content against these n-grams and fails the commit.
# It is a fingerprint list, not the corpus: each line is a distinctive fragment, sufficient
# to detect the text without reproducing it.
#
# Publishing the instrument is not fixed by scrubbing after the fact -- a git history keeps
# what a working tree forgets. The check runs before the commit, every time.
#
# DECISION 2026-09-12 (Ian): THE TEN PLAINTEXT FRAGMENTS STAY.
#
# The question was raised because they are ten verbatim propositions published in a public
# repository by the very file that exists to keep the instrument out of it, and check_corpus
# now supports --hashed-only for a tree that would rather not ship them.
#
# They stay for the reader. Someone auditing this gate has to be able to see what it matches
# on; a file of truncated digests is unfalsifiable by inspection. Ten fragments of sixty-two
# propositions is the disclosure a reviewer needs and is not a republication of the instrument.
#
# THE FUTURE-VERSION IDEA, recorded here so it is not lost: a HELD-BACK CORPUS. If a later
# version wants to measure a model that has not already read the test, the instrument has to
# stop being public before the models are trained on it. That argues for a held instrument
# with only hashes published -- the opposite trade from this file -- and it is a v3 design
# question, not a reason to change v2 now. Contamination of the measuring device by the thing
# being measured is a real threat to any published benchmark and this study has not addressed it.

